\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Random slope.} The generalized-pivotal-quantity (GPQ) interval of
Oliva-Aviles & Hauser (2025), for both targets (\code{over = "observation"} and
\code{"group_mean"}), two-sided and one-sided, on every fit (singular included).
It is a Monte-Carlo interval: set \code{lmm_interval_control(seed = )} for
reproducibility (see Details); the default \code{M = 20000} is the size
validated by simulation. \code{attr(., "diagnostics")$route_reason} is \code{"auto"}, or
\code{"method"} when \code{method = "gpq"} was passed. The REML closed-form interval
(\code{method = "reml-mls"}) was removed in 1.0.0 because it under-covers; it
now signals an error of class \code{reintervals_error_defunct}.
\item \strong{Random intercept + fixed slope.} Clean balanced -> EMS engine; single-
observation / staggered / unbalanced -> the Montes ANOVA/MLS closed form.
\item \strong{Intercept-only one-way} (\code{y ~ 1 + (1 | g)}, one grouping factor, no
covariates), balanced or not -> the Hoffman & Kringle (2005) closed form
(\code{"anova-mls"}). \code{method = "ems-mls"} gives the Francq et
al. (2019) interval instead; the two coincide on balanced data with a
non-singular fit and differ on singular or unbalanced data.
\item \strong{Any other random-intercept model, no fixed slope} (one-way with
covariates, nested, crossed) -> EMS engine.
\item \strong{\code{method = "gpq"} on a random-intercept model} (never chosen by
\code{"auto"}; \strong{status: under validation (step 13b)}, every call signals a
warning of class \code{reintervals_warning_unvalidated}). The same GPQ
engine, on the random-intercept members of the Oliva-Aviles & Hauser
(2025) class (their sections 4.1 and 4.3); on the one-way model its
pivots are those of Liao, Lin & Iyer (2005). Eligible fits: exactly one
grouping factor with a random intercept only (\code{(1 | g)}); any fixed
effects (time, covariates, factors), provided the columns other than
the intercept, \code{X0}, together with the lot indicators \code{Z} form a matrix
\verb{W = (X0, Z)} of full column rank (the paper's conditions (a) and (b);
a covariate constant within every lot fails it); no prior weights; and
a positive residual df \code{n - q - K} (\code{q} = number of columns of \code{X0}, \code{K}
= number of lots). Single-observation lots are allowed. Other designs
(two or more random factors: nested, crossed) signal
\code{reintervals_error_method}; a non-positive residual df signals
\code{reintervals_error_residual_df}. Both targets are available:
\code{over = "observation"} (content distribution \code{N(theta, tau2 + s2e)}) and
\code{over = "group_mean"} (\code{N(theta, tau2)}, the distribution of lot means;
for a random-intercept model this target exists only with
\code{method = "gpq"}). The rows carry \code{method = "gpq"} and the GPQ
diagnostics (\code{gpq_seed}, \code{rng_kind}, \code{mc_se_lower}, \code{mc_se_upper}, \code{M},
\code{n_na_draws}, ...). Unlike the closed forms, the interval accounts for
the uncertainty of the predicted mean through its GPQ.
}

For slope models the between-group variance \code{V_G(t0)} is quadratic in the
time covariate, so evaluating at a \code{t0} outside the observed time range is
an extrapolation of that quadratic. The interval is still computed, but every
verb (\code{\link[=ti_lmm]{ti_lmm()}}, \code{\link[=ci_lmm]{ci_lmm()}}, \code{\link[=pi_lmm]{pi_lmm()}}, \code{\link[=new_group_mean_lmm]{new_group_mean_lmm()}},
\code{\link[=lmm_predict]{lmm_predict()}}) and the raw engines \code{\link[=ti_gpq_raw]{ti_gpq_raw()}} and \code{\link[=ti_anova_raw]{ti_anova_raw()}} signal
one warning of class \code{reintervals_warning_extrapolation} per call when any
requested \code{t0} lies outside the observed time range, naming those \code{t0} values
and the range: the interval assumes the linear (fixed-slope) model, or the
quadratic-in-\code{t0} between-group variance (random slope), holds beyond the
data. For slope models \code{newdata} must contain the time variable as a numeric,
finite, non-missing column (otherwise an error of class
\code{reintervals_error_input} naming the variable); other columns are ignored.

\strong{Monte-Carlo (GPQ) intervals.} Within one call, the GPQ pivot draws are
generated once and reused for every time point in \code{newdata} and for both
limits (common random numbers): for a given seed and \code{M}, the limits at a
time point are identical whatever other time points are requested, and in
any order. The seed used is in \code{attr(., "diagnostics")$gpq_seed} and the
RNG kind in \verb{$rng_kind}; passing
\verb{control = lmm_interval_control(seed = <gpq_seed>, M = <same M>)} under the
same RNG kind reproduces the interval exactly (with \code{seed = NULL}, one seed
is drawn from the caller's RNG stream, so \code{set.seed()} before the call also
reproduces it; see \code{\link[=lmm_interval_control]{lmm_interval_control()}}). The diagnostics also carry the
Monte-Carlo standard error of each limit, \code{mc_se_lower} and \code{mc_se_upper}
(\code{NA} on the infinite side of a one-sided interval), computed by MC
sections (\code{mc_sections = 20} consecutive groups of draws; the standard
error is the standard deviation of the 20 section limits over
\code{sqrt(20)}). The MC SE is the run-to-run jitter of the Monte-Carlo
computation, not statistical uncertainty: a limit within approximately 2 MC
SE of a specification limit has a seed-dependent pass/fail. In a sleepstudy
check at \code{M = 2000} the sectioning estimate ran about 8\% below the observed
run-to-run SD (sections of 100 draws); the gap is expected to shrink at the
default \code{M = 20000}.

\strong{Vectorised calls.} \code{P}, \code{level} (or \code{alpha}) and \code{sides} accept vectors;
the result is their full cross product with the time points (see Value).
On the GPQ route one call draws the pivots once and solves for the
between-lot variance once per time point, then evaluates every content,
confidence and side on those same draws, each row with its own Monte-Carlo
SE. With an explicit \code{seed}, each row is identical to the scalar call with
that \code{P}, \code{level} and \code{sides}. With \code{seed = NULL} a vectorised call draws
one seed (one draw set) for all its rows, whereas separate scalar calls each
draw their own seed, so the equality holds only with an explicit seed. The
closed-form engines compute each combination with their scalar code.
Warnings are signalled once per call. Every GPQ row (both routes, the
GPQ new-group-mean arm and \code{\link[=ti_gpq_raw]{ti_gpq_raw()}}) reports the number of draws \code{M}
in the diagnostics.

On a random-slope model the GPQ route requires
every lot to have at least two distinct time points and otherwise signals
an error of class \code{reintervals_error_time_points} (the random-intercept GPQ
has no such requirement). The GPQ interval works
from the per-lot OLS fits of the raw data and ignores the fitted
random-effects structure: on an independent (\code{||}) random-slope fit it is the
same as on the correlated (unstructured) fit of the same data.
}
\section{Known limitation (one-sided GPQ limits)}{

On one-sided GPQ tolerance-interval rows (both routes, and \code{\link[=ti_gpq_raw]{ti_gpq_raw()}})
a warning of class \code{reintervals_warning_limitation} is signalled, once per
call, in two cases (the interval itself is unchanged):
\itemize{
\item \strong{confidence below 0.95}, either target: in validation (step 13,
random-slope designs), one-sided GPQ limits at 90\% confidence covered
0.879 to 0.90 (median 0.894 to 0.903);
\item \strong{confidence 0.95 or above with \code{over = "group_mean"}} (\code{c_resid = 0} in
\code{\link[=ti_gpq_raw]{ti_gpq_raw()}}): in validation (step 13), one-sided batch-mean GPQ limits
at 95\% confidence covered 0.936 to 0.95 (median 0.948).
}

Two-sided GPQ intervals are not affected at these levels. The figures come
from random-slope designs; on the random-intercept GPQ route
(\code{method = "gpq"}, unvalidated, with its own
\code{reintervals_warning_unvalidated}) the limitation warning applies as well.
Each GPQ row records \code{attr(., "diagnostics")$limitation_flag}:
\code{"gpq_one_sided_lowconf"}, \code{"gpq_one_sided_group_mean"}, or \code{NA} (every
two-sided row, and one-sided rows outside both cases).
}

\section{Known limitation (two-sided MLS tolerance interval under extrapolation)}{

\strong{Provisional until step 13b.} On random-intercept models the two-sided
\code{ems-mls} and \code{anova-mls} intervals (auto or forced) can under-cover when
the predicted mean is uncertain relative to the estimated total variance
and that total variance is well-determined, typically at an evaluation
point beyond the observed times with a small between-lot variance. Every
two-sided row of these routes reports in \code{attr(., "diagnostics")}:
\itemize{
\item \code{ti_R}, the variance of the predicted mean at the evaluation point over
the estimated total variance: on \code{ems-mls} rows
\code{var_mean / sigma2_t} (\verb{var_mean = x0' V x0}, the fixed-effect variance of
the predicted mean; \verb{sigma2_t = sum_j k_j EMS_j}); on \code{anova-mls} rows
\code{1 / n_E = VhatYh / Vhat_Y} (the Montes / Hoffman-Kringle variance of the
centre over the content variance);
\item \code{ti_df_T}, the Satterthwaite df of \code{sigma2_t} from the expected-mean-square
decomposition, \verb{(sum_j k_j EMS_j)^2 / sum_j (k_j EMS_j)^2 / df_j}, on
either route (\code{NA} when the decomposition is unavailable);
\item \code{ti_warn_fired}, \code{TRUE} when \code{ti_R > ti_warn_R} and
\code{ti_df_T >= ti_warn_dfT} (\code{\link[=lmm_interval_control]{lmm_interval_control()}}; defaults 0.25 and 40);
\item \code{rerouted} and \code{reroute_reason} (see \code{reroute} in
\code{\link[=lmm_interval_control]{lmm_interval_control()}}): \code{FALSE} / \code{NA} unless \code{reroute = TRUE}.
}

One-sided rows carry \code{NA} (\code{reroute_reason = "not_applicable"}), as do the
GPQ routes (random slope, \code{method = "gpq"}). When some row has
\code{ti_warn_fired = TRUE}, one warning of class \code{reintervals_warning_limitation}
(condition field \code{limitation = "ti_mls_extrapolation"}) is signalled per
call; the interval is unchanged. In the step-13 training data (simulation,
\code{R > 0.25} and \code{df_T_hat >= 40}, per-cell coverage on the replicates where
it fires) the interval covered 0.76-0.88 at a confidence of 0.90 and
0.84-0.95 at 0.95. The thresholds and ranges were derived in-sample and are
being tested on fresh seeds in step 13b.
}

\section{Availability}{

Tolerance intervals require a closed-form variance decomposition. They are
available for one-way, nested, and crossed-with-interaction random-intercept
designs, the fixed-slope stability design, and random-slope models. For a
design without such a decomposition (two crossed factors with no interaction
term, or four or more grouping factors), \code{\link[=ti_lmm]{ti_lmm()}} and
\code{\link[=new_group_mean_lmm]{new_group_mean_lmm()}} signal an error; \code{\link[=ci_lmm]{ci_lmm()}} and \code{\link[=pi_lmm]{pi_lmm()}} remain
available for those models.
}

\examples{
# intercept-only one-way -> Hoffman-Kringle (anova-mls)
fh <- lme4::lmer(conc ~ 1 + (1 | run), data = hk_assay)
ti_lmm(fh, P = 0.95, level = 0.90)
# random intercept + fixed slope (clean balanced) -> EMS engine
fi <- lme4::lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (1 | Subject), data = lme4::sleepstudy)
ti_lmm(fi, newdata = data.frame(Days = 0))
# random slope -> GPQ (both targets). M = 2000 keeps the example fast; the
# default M = 20000 is the validated size.
fs <- lme4::lmer(Reaction ~ Days + (1 + Days | Subject), data = lme4::sleepstudy)
ti_lmm(fs, newdata = data.frame(Days = 5),
       control = lmm_interval_control(seed = 1, M = 2000))
ti_lmm(fs, newdata = data.frame(Days = 5), over = "group_mean",
       control = lmm_interval_control(seed = 1, M = 2000))
}
